{
 "question": "Does the batch penalty follow `B % 4 == 0`, or the vector width XLA picks? The two differ only when L is even, and every window measured before this one contained no even non-multiple of four.",
 "rule": {
  "elements": "B * L * N^2 * 3, and N is always odd, so v2(elements) = v2(B) + v2(L)",
  "width": "XLA launches 128 threads and gives each one min(4, 2^v2(elements)) elements",
  "cost": "only width 4 is expensive. Width 2 costs +0.1% against width 1 (measured on L=3, where one lattice spans all three widths), so the penalty condition collapses to a single inequality: v2(B) + v2(L) >= 2.",
  "consequences": {
   "L odd": "width 4 only at B divisible by 4 -> the mod-4 penalty",
   "L = 2 mod 4": "width 4 at every even B -> the mod-4 rule MISCLASSIFIES",
   "L divisible by 4": "width 4 at every B -> flat, immune"
  }
 },
 "findings": [
  "89 grid values across three lattices, 0 mispredicted, from each module's own thunk_sequence.txt. Block size is 128 everywhere; only the grid moves.",
  "L=6: the width-4 split separates perfectly (+14.7%, no overlap) while `B % 4` does not (+10.2%, overlap). B=58 and B=62 are expensive, which no earlier window could have seen.",
  "L=3: the width-4 split and the mod-4 split coincide, +14.2%, no overlap.",
  "L=3 also rules out a graded cost: B=58 (width 2) sits between its width-1 neighbours 57 and 59 (2.470 / 2.484 / 2.502), and B=62 between 61 and 63 (2.366 / 2.373 / 2.367). The 2-wide path is free.",
  "L=4: every cell is width 4, so no split exists and none is seen (mod-4 -2.5% with overlap). Immunity is not the penalty failing to apply, it is the penalty already paid in every cell.",
  "Fusion names are not stable across B -- `loop_select_fusion_2` walks the full array at B=58 and the per-site arrays elsewhere, and `loop_maximum_negate_fusion_1` exists only at B=60 -- so grids must be scored per entry, not per kernel name."
 ],
 "open_questions": [
  "Why width 4 costs and width 2 does not. Four floats is 16 B per thread; transaction size is the obvious suspect but it is untested.",
  "Why no penalty appears at the ~2 MB footprint (STRIDE_SWEEP was null at every v2), while 5 MB separates cleanly.",
  "Whether the earlier mod-8 structure is a wider path at higher v2. The widths seen here stop at 4."
 ],
 "supersedes": "the claim in mod4_cause_xla_vectorisation.json that the choice is binary between a scalar and a 4-wide path. There is also a 2-wide path at v2(elements) == 1. It changes no stated rule, because the 2-wide path carries no measurable cost.",
